// --- yad-ephraim (1) ---
patchFile('siman_020/yad-ephraim/part-001.txt', 'yad-ephraim', {
  '1#_': `<b>And it was slaughtered with it a treifah.</b> See Ba'er Heitev; and he wrote in Peri Megadim ch. 1 s.k. 1 regarding one who slaughtered a calf: he began close to the head itself from the trachea and slaughtered diagonally, and in that same diagonal he also slaughtered the esophagus, and they brought the head split in two—since it is fitting that the cut in the esophagus that lay in it was longer than the grasp of a hand in width, there is to validate—see there; and see in the responsum he brought in Peri Megadim and answered on it. And his view is that the crop is only the place attached to the cheeks themselves, and if something more is seen to the eyes it is permitted—see there; and see in Chokhmat Shlomo. And he wrote in Shevut Yaakov ch. 1 siman 48: a slaughterer who is left-handed and trains his hands to slaughter does not slaughter in the exact middle of the neck but from the sides—that is, diagonally—and nevertheless he cuts the simanim and does not touch the spinal column; and some people murmur about him that he should be removed—and he answered that even according to Tosafot who are stringent about slaughtering from the sides—that is when he also cuts the spinal column etc.—unlike here, where he slants sideways to slaughter diagonally and cuts only the simanim; Tosafot too agree it is permitted l'chatchila, and even if you say l'chatchila one should not do so, nevertheless one does not remove him for this—see there.`,
});

// --- turei-zahav (8) ---
patchFile('siman_020/turei-zahav/part-001.txt', 'turei-zahav', {
  '1#א': `<b>That is at the end of the trachea, etc.</b> Thus wrote Kol Bo in the name of Rav Bar Tzeloni: the matter of hat slope is that opposite the large ring there is flesh there, and the mouth of the flesh is made like wheat grains, and upon those grains there is a covering similar to a hadas leaf, and upon it one bone from the large ring surrounds this flesh like a hat; and in the middle of this bone there is one slope in the body of the bone and it is called hat slope; and the sages said: from hat slope downward is kosher, for it is about a finger above the large ring—end of his words.`,
  '1#ב': `<b>And l'chatchila he shall slaughter, etc.</b> It implies that b'dieved it is kosher even above until hat slope; and so wrote Maharshal—that this measure of the large ring is for distancing because of concern for mishap, and in a place of great loss one must distinguish accordingly.`,
  '1#ג': `<b>Without forcing itself.</b> In the Gemara it is stated: Rava said, provided that it does not force itself—Rashi explained: to pull the simanim away; we require human hands; Rabbi Chanina asked: if it forced itself—what? Rashi explained: that it stretched its neck excessively at the time of shechitah; teiku—and for stringency. And Tosafot explained this ask: it forced itself—that it stretched its head more than necessary to take a vegetable from a pit—end of their words. It appears their intent is that they are not pleased to explain Rashi's explanation about the time of shechitah, for if it is more than what is normal to stretch when it grazes, it is certainly invalid since we already established the measure is when it grazes; therefore they explained that the doubt is in the explanation of "whenever it grazes"—for it is an animal's manner to stretch its head more to take a vegetable from a pit and afterward eat after it returns somewhat from the great stretch—shall we say this too is included in the measure they said "when it grazes," or shall we say only when it grazes in its simple sense and stretching to take the vegetable is not included in this—as explained, it is correct; and with this it is resolved why Tur did not bring that there is no distinction between forcing by a person and forcing by itself in shechitah.`,
  '2#א': `<b>When one cuts it, it contracts.</b> That is not the explanation that one part widens from another, this above and that below as some slaughterers understand—a thing that applies everywhere one cuts in living flesh; rather the explanation is that the skin wrinkles and makes folds in the place of shechitah and the cavity is covered, unlike in the crop where the cavity remains exposed and does not wrinkle.`,
  '2#ב': `<b>And there is no difference between a dove, etc.</b> He wrote this because in the Gemara "yonah"—R' Zeira said until Mivlatena—and it appears from Beit Yosef that he explains yonah literally, meaning a dove has a different law from other birds, and Rama wondered in Darkei Moshe that yonah is the name of an amora; and so Maharshal.`,
  '2#ג': `<b>Until the zefek.</b> Meaning the roof of the zefek; and Beit Yosef wrote siman 20 to distinguish: if he slaughtered on the roof of the zefek, if white skin is seen it is known he touched it, because the red is more common in the esophagus from outside than the white; and the white is longer than the red; and another sign: on the roof of the zefek there are not two skins as in the esophagus.`,
  '3#_': `<b>And know that he cut them, etc.</b> From his words it appears there are two things: returning the simanim and knowing the cutting in the simanim before the spinal column; and he learned thus from Ra'osh who wrote on the mishnah "one who slaughters from the sides—his shechitah is kosher, provided he knows he cut the simanim before he cut the spinal column because the simanim are soft and pushed aside by the knife"—end of his words. It implies that even if he returned, he must know he cut the simanim before the spinal column; so too Beit Yosef; and therefore he wrote here that two are required. And I wonder—for then Ra'osh should have mentioned the law of returning and written upon it that knowledge too is required; rather it is clear that the return itself is the knowledge, for by returning he certainly knows he cut the simanim before the spinal column; and so it appears to me from Tosafot who wrote "the mishnah mentions one who slaughters from the sides is kosher specifically b'dieved; even if he returned, we decree lest he not return"—accordingly, if it were that returning does not suffice, why decree lest he not return—let it be invalid even with return, lest he not have cut the simanim before the spinal column; rather plainly that cutting the simanim first is since he returned, and with this he knew that matter; and so Agudah: returning alone is kosher. And for us there is no nafka mina from what we said, for the main concern here is lest he cut the spinal column before the simanim, and we hold it is forbidden even if he cut the spinal column after the simanim, as written in siman 24; and it turns out the kosher status is only without cutting the spinal column; nevertheless kosher even without return, for we have no concern—as explained. Rather Maharshal introduced one stringency: any time he cut flesh of the neck before the simanim there is to forbid, and this is from concern for hesech—lest when he began to slaughter in the flesh he touched the esophagus slightly and returned and cut in the flesh—and he derived this from Rashba in teshuvah haketzarah etc. "from the sides his slaughter is kosher because the simanim are slaughtered before he cut the spinal column and the flesh upon them"—end; from "and the flesh upon them" it implies if he cut in the flesh first there is concern for perforation of the esophagus; and in my humble opinion there is no place for this stringency, for then we should be concerned from doubt in every slaughterer lest in the middle of slaughtering the simanim he cut in the flesh and returned to the simanim, or lest when he began to cut in the skin he touched the siman and returned and cut in the skin. And furthermore I found explicitly in Rashba Mishmeret HaBayit daf 26a at the end etc.: what he cut in the flesh does not affect; when he reached the simanim and slaughtered two-thirds, according to all kosher—end; behold before you Rashba clearly that we are not concerned for cutting in flesh at the time of slaughter; and that which he mentioned in the brief teshuvah "and the flesh upon them" is for another reason—for he holds there is no concern lest he cut the entire spinal column before cutting the simanim, automatically it would first be forbidden, since there is flesh on the spinal column certainly the cutting of all the simanim will finish before the cutting of the flesh and entire spinal column finishes; and so explained in Tur HaTeshuvot: "the reason of the matter is that even though he slaughters from the side and cuts the spinal column with slaughtering the simanim, slaughter of the simanim precedes cutting the entire spinal column; and even treifah is not until he slaughters the simanim"—end; this is explained as I said, that he means: not only is concern for nevelah obvious there is none—for that is what Ze'ira said "the spinal column broke and majority of flesh with it is nevelah," for certainly he will not afterward need to come to cutting majority of flesh; rather even concern for treifah—that is, without majority of flesh—there is none here, since in any case he must cut a little in flesh before cutting spinal column and simanim, and simanim are slaughtered before a cut is made in flesh and spinal column. But he does not deal with prohibition of hesech—for certainly there is no measure of hesech here; and even for us who are stringent in all hesechot this is only if he raised the knife even slightly—in this we decree "he raised and paused a little" because of "pausing greatly"; but when he does not raise at all, why should we be more stringent for us than the sages of the Talmud—this is not subject to decree and proof for speech, for behold in slaughtering in two or three places in one circuit it is kosher even for us, and we should not say to be stringent for us because of pausing a little, which is routine every day—accordingly there is stringency only when he raises slightly; and see what I wrote in siman 21 seif 4. This is Rashba's intent; and see what I wrote further on this in siman 27. But from hesech they were not strict even if within the slaughter he cuts the spinal column, and how much more so flesh of the neck—there is no measure; and with this for us too it is permitted, for specifically if he raised the knife even slightly we are stringent because of "do not distinguish," but not here—as written in siman 23 seif 2 on this, see there; and what is written in the next seif—and it is from the words of Kol Bo—that simanim should present themselves before flesh of the neck: his main concern is lest he come to cut the spinal column, as he concluded there explicitly "and to cut the spinal column, etc."; and therefore they were also strict about flesh of the neck that is on the spinal column because of distancing from the unseemly; and on the contrary, from there is proof—since he found no reason for prohibition except because of the spinal column and not because of hesech in slaughter even without cutting the spinal column, but certainly there is no concern at all as we said.`,
  '4#_': `<b>It is good to be careful.</b> Meaning: even when slaughtering opposite the throat.`,
});

// --- siftei-kohen (8) ---
patchFile('siman_020/siftei-kohen/part-001.txt', 'siftei-kohen', {
  '1#א': `<b>And that is at the end of the trachea, etc.</b> And the view of Mahariu appears not to validate above the large ring; and so Mahari"l; and so in Hagahot Mahariu; and so Agur on Ashkenazi shechitah; and so he saw practiced his teacher his father, may he rest in peace; and so Maharshal in his hagahot on shechitot—that we do not practice validating at all above the large ring because of concern for mishaps; and so in recent shechitot; even though Bach wondered—behold it is explained in chapter 1 of Chullin that it is kosher; and in my humble opinion they derived thus from the incident of hagramah in chapter HaGozel (Bava Kamma daf 99b)—it implies there that because of avodah zarah there is reason to be stringent not to validate above the large ring, as Tosafot there—see there; however Bach concludes in a place of great loss and the like one should rule as the Mechaber wrote; and so ruled Maharshal in shechitot.`,
  '1#ב': `<b>Wing of the lung.</b> That it is on the left side; and when the animal grazes it protrudes more from the body than the right side, which is thicker—end of Hagahot Maimoniyot that Beit Yosef brought; Darkei Moshe.`,
  '1#ג': `<b>Without forcing itself.</b> At the time of shechitah to stretch its neck excessively.`,
  '1#ד': `<b>And in a bird below in the trachea, etc.</b> Thus wrote Darkei Moshe; and in Ran chapter Eizehu Mekoman daf 128a he wrote: and Ra'ah received from his brothers that the measure of the esophagus in a bird is opposite it in the trachea—end; and therefore it is recorded here in Shulchan Aruch "Ran in the name of Ra'ah"; and it is an error—for thus said Ran: measure of the esophagus of a bird is above opposite it in the trachea from hat slope downward; and so it is explicitly in his words, and Beit Yosef brings it; and so in Ra'ah in Beit HaBayit daf 25a; if so, tzarich iyun where Rav Berachot derived this "below in the trachea like the esophagus"; and it appears to me it came out to him from what Beit Yosef wrote that in a bird below is the esophagus until the roof of the zefek, and in the trachea until opposite the roof of the zefek; and below in s.k. 7 it is explained that the measure of the esophagus below until the zefek that Tur wrote means the roof of the zefek.`,
  '2#א': `<b>His slaughter is invalid.</b> All this is the language of the Rambam; and K'li Muor wrote on it etc.: it implies it is nevelah, for wherever we said in shechitah "invalid" it is nevelah, and if one eats from it a kezayit he is lashed for eating nevelah; and it is difficult—for the crop of the esophagus and the stomach are only treifah and not nevelah; and one can say that therefore he did not say "it is nevelah" and "it imparts tumah in a burden," but only "if one ate from it a kezayit he is lashed"; but for tumah he did not descend; and because most of those mentioned in these chapters are nevelah, he used the language nevelah—end. And I do not know what troubled him, for he wrote explicitly "and lashed for eating nevelah"; rather certainly—even though perforation of the crop (and see siman 33 seif 3) and stomach are only treifah—nevertheless since it was disqualified and perforated in shechitah, shechitah caused it to become invalid and it is a complete nevelah, as we taught explicitly in chapter HaShochet: anything shechitah causes to become invalid is nevelah—for certainly every treifah, if it were not slaughtered and died, is nevelah; therefore since he slaughtered in the crop of the esophagus and stomach it became treifah and the shechitah does not permit it, for this is not a place of shechitah; and there is no greater nevelah than this—and this is simple. And there is a nafka mina for us in what is nevelah regarding oteh v'et beno, as written above siman 16 end.`,
  '2#ב': `<b>In animal and beast, etc.</b> This too is the language of the Rambam; and it implies he holds that all animals and beasts are equal in this measure, only in a bird according to its value large and small; and therefore Bach wondered on Tur, who after the Rambam's measure—which is in a large ox and the rest of animal, beast, and bird according to large and small—his words are not aligned, for Tosafot and Ra'osh did not write thus but after Rashi's measure, which is four fingers—end. And Rashba too wrote in teshuvah ha'arokhah after the Rambam's measure—and this in animal and beast, but in a bird etc.; and Bach understood that Bachya's complaints are on what Tur wrote "and in a bird according to large and small," and therefore wondered on him, for behold in the words of Rambam and Rashba it is explained as Tur wrote; and this is that behold immediately after Bachya wrote "and Rambam in chapter 1 wrote as the explanation of BeHaG, and wrote that this is the measure in animal and beast, but in a bird everything according to large and small"—end; Beit Yosef—behold explicitly his intent as said after. I found in Derishah that he explained explicitly Bachya's view as my words; and see s.k. 4.`,
  '2#ג': `<b>And below until the zefek.</b> Meaning the separation; and Bach—that is, until the roof of the zefek, and the roof is not included; and so Ra'osh chapter Eizehu Mekoman; and Maharshal there siman 6; and Mahariu.`,
  '3#_': `<b>And if he slaughtered from the sides, etc.</b> Thus wrote Beit Yosef; and it appears from the words of Tosafot that if he did not return the simanim it is invalid—for thus they wrote: "one who slaughters from the sides—we find to say specifically b'dieved; but l'chatchila, even if he returned the simanim, we decree lest he not return"; and Ra'osh wrote: "provided he knows he cut the simanim before he cut the spinal column, for the simanim are soft and pushed aside by the knife"; and it appears from his words that even if he returned he must know; and even if he did not return, as long as he knows—it is kosher—end; and he brings it in Darkei Moshe; and what he wrote that it appears from Tosafot that if he did not return it is invalid—in my humble opinion it is so, for if without return it were kosher, the decree "lest he not return" would not apply, since even without return itself it is kosher b'dieved and mishap does not come from this; and we do not find anywhere a decree like this; and so agreed in Sefer Torat Chaim; and so in Sefer Zivchei Tzedek on the end of Tosafot and Mordechai; and I cannot descend to the end of the view of Maharash Idelstein in his chiddushim and Bach who challenged Beit Yosef on this; and that which they challenged—behold the mishnah teaches at the end "one who slaughters from the nape—his slaughter is invalid," and the Gemara establishes it specifically without return; if so, what difference is there between the beginning and the end? It is not a difficulty—for at the end his slaughter is always invalid since he slaughters from the nape, which is not so in the beginning, which deals specifically with knowing—as Ra'osh and Tur and Rabbeinu Yerucham wrote: kosher even without return; only Tosafot say that even if he returned, l'chatchila we decree "lest he not return" from the default case, for then it is invalid—as Ra'osh and his supporters wrote; and so wrote Agudah chapter 1 of Chullin siman 25 explicitly etc.: "one who slaughters from the sides—his slaughter is kosher specifically b'dieved, and he should return the simanim"—end; behold without return even b'dieved it is invalid; and this is specifically from the default case. However, what Beit Yosef wrote that it appears from Ra'osh's words that even if he returned he must know—in my humble opinion it is not so at all, for it is not reasonable to invalidate since he returned the simanim and certainly cut them before the spinal column; and behold Rashi and Ran chapter 1 of Chullin validate even without return—even if he did not know—because certainly from the default case he slaughtered them before the spinal column; and so appears the view of Rashba in teshuvah ha'arokhah daf 26a; if so, why should we say Ra'osh argues—a distant dispute like this? Rather it appears that therefore Ra'osh explained "provided he knows"—because the Gemara establishes the mishnah without return; but he agrees that with return it is kosher in the default case, as Agudah holds (and as is proven from Tosafot who wrote "lest he not return" since they did not say "lest the return itself," and understand); and so it appears from the words of Tur and Rabbeinu Yerucham, who wrote "and if he returned the simanim behind the nape—it is kosher," and they did not condition knowing as they conditioned in the beginning; rather certainly as I said—and understand well, for all this in my humble opinion is clear: that now Tosafot, Ra'osh, his supporters, and Agudah stand on one view; only it is difficult on the Mechaber in what appears from his words that knowing alone does not help—this is against the Gemara that establishes the mishnah without return; and in any case knowing helps (and according to what is written in Sefer Torat Chaim it is possible the beginning of the mishnah deals specifically with return—but his words are very forced—see there and understand). And one must say that what the Mechaber wrote "and know" is a vav of division, meaning: and that is, he returned or he knew; and what he wrote afterward "and the same applies to one who slaughters from the nape" refers only to return; and the words of Pri Chadash cannot be reconciled thus—see there; and see in siman 24 seif 5 and what is written there.`,
});

// --- baer-heitev (7) ---
patchFile('siman_020/baer-heitev/part-001.txt', 'baer-heitev', {
  '1#א': `<b>It.</b> Meaning on the left side; and when the animal grazes it protrudes more from the body than the right, which is thicker; and Shach wrote that one should not validate above the large ring; and so is the view of Maharshal—even though Bach does not permit except in a place of great loss, one should be lenient as the Mechaber's view; and so in Ashkenazi shechitah.`,
  '1#ב': `<b>Excessively.</b> Ba'er Heitev wrote that above in the trachea in a bird he did not mention explicitly in Shulchan Aruch what its law is; but from the implication of the language it appears its law is also from hat slope downward like an animal.`,
  '2#א': `<b>Like the stomach.</b> Taz explained that the skin wrinkles and makes folds in the place of shechitah and the cavity is covered—unlike in the crop, where the cavity remains exposed and does not wrinkle.`,
  '2#ב': `<b>Invalid.</b> And Shach wrote in the name of the Rambam that it is nevelah, for we establish that anything shechitah causes to become invalid is nevelah; and there is a nafka mina for us whether it is nevelah regarding oteh v'et beno, as above siman 16 end.`,
  '2#ג': `<b>His fingers.</b> And Shach wrote: and all animals and beasts are equal, whether large or small.`,
  '2#ד': `<b>The zefek.</b> And Shach explained: until the roofs of the zefek, and the roof is not included; and a sign: if he slaughtered on the roof of the zefek, if white skin is seen it is known he touched it; and another sign: on the roof of the zefek there are not two skins as in the esophagus.`,
  '3#_': `<b>The knife.</b> And Shach wrote: from the Mechaber's words it appears two are required—returning simanim and also knowing he cut them before the spinal column; and in truth this is against the Gemara—for certainly with return alone it also helps; and one must say this "and know" is a vav of division, meaning: or he knew; and certainly from the default case, if he did not return, there is to forbid. And Taz wrote: for us there is no nafka mina at all in this law, for the main concern here is lest he cut the spinal column before the simanim, and we hold it is forbidden even if he cut the spinal column after the simanim, as written in siman 24 seif 7. And what Bach wrote that if he cut flesh of the neck before the simanim there is to forbid from concern lest when he begins to slaughter in the flesh he touches the esophagus slightly and returns and cuts in the flesh—Taz disagrees and brought proof for his words; also one need not be concerned for any aspect of prohibition of hesech as long as he did not raise the knife—end. A slaughterer who is left-handed and trains his hands to slaughter does not slaughter exactly in the middle of the neck but diagonally; and nevertheless he cuts the simanim and does not touch the spinal column—it is permitted even l'chatchila, and it is obvious one should not remove him for this. Teshuvat She'eilot siman 48.`,
});

// --- beur-hagra (8) ---
patchFile('siman_020/beur-hagra/part-001.txt', 'beur-hagra', {
  '1#א': `<b>And if he did not leave any, etc.</b> As Tosafot explain there: he hit and did not touch above.`,
  '1#ב': `<b>And l'chatchila, etc.</b> From Tosafot Bava Kamma 99b s.v. min'ach etc.; Or Zarua etc.`,
  '1#ג': `<b>When they inflate it, etc.</b> As written above siman 35 seif 8 in the gloss; and all these matters etc.`,
  '2#א': `<b>And the measure, etc.; and some say, etc.; and in a bird, etc.</b> From Tosafot there s.v. kedei etc.`,
  '2#ב': `<b>And there is no, etc.</b> Meaning: not as Tosafot [read] "yonah," R' Zeira said—referring to yonah.`,
  '2#ג': `<b>And below, etc.</b> From there onward it is called zefek; and see there one who said etc.; and Ravina said etc.; why when he slaughters in a vineyard etc.`,
  '3#א': `<b>Must, etc.</b> From Tosafot there s.v. hashochet etc.`,
  '3#ב': `<b>And that is, he returned, etc.</b> Thus they inferred from the words of Tosafot cited above, who said a decree lest he not return; and if b'dieved kosher, the decree would not apply; and what the Gemara says "and if you would think specifically with return" etc.; and the mishnah there—with knowing he cut simanim before the spinal column; then return on the sides is not needed, unlike the nape where it is impossible; and what Shulchan Aruch wrote "and know" means: or he knew; and what he wrote "and the same applies to one who slaughters from the nape" refers only to return—Shach. But the main thing is as the view of teshuvah ha'arokhah and Ran: from the sides kosher even without return, as the sugya there; and he wrote in teshuvah ha'arokhah there that even l'chatchila it is permitted even without return, even though he also cuts the spinal column—nevertheless it does not become treifah from this; and he did not mention b'dieved except because of melikah, for even b'dieved it is invalid; but Ran and Rambam wrote b'dieved specifically—see there; and Rashi wrote as the words of teshuvah ha'arokhah.`,
});

// --- kereti (12) ---
patchFile('siman_020/kereti/part-001.txt', 'kereti', {
  '1#א': `Within the ring—this is the language of the Rambam in his explanation of the mishnah; from this it implies the wheat grains are within the ring; and if he slaughtered in the wheat grains he has not yet been derore above the ring. And in the Gemara it implies the wheat grains are above the large ring; and if he slaughtered in the wheat grains he has already passed the large ring upward. And see in Kol Bo siman 14 who wrote one should stand on this. And what appears to me after investigation and examination with slaughterers is thus: when the animal eats and walks bent over, the wheat grains are within the ring and bend into it—and with this the words of Rav Rambam fit; but when it stretches the neck at the time of shechitah as those being slaughtered do, then it pulls the wheat grains upward toward hat slope and toward the head—and then it is above the ring.`,
  '1#ב': `And l'chatchila thus agreed Mahariu and Mahari"l; and see Peleti what I wrote their reason from the Gemara.`,
  '1#ג': `Head of the wing of the lung—meaning on the left side, for it rises above more than the right wing—Shach and Peri Chadash.`,
  '1#ד': `Without forcing itself; and if it forced itself and he slaughtered—behold this is doubtful nevelah; and if a person forced it, it appears from the Rambam that it is also doubtful; and Peri Chadash wrote certainly; and see Peleti that I argued for the Rambam.`,
  '2#א': `His slaughter is invalid—and this means nevelah; and the latter authorities raised Shach and Peri Chadash that it deals with one who did not slaughter it after shechitah but left the animal or bird until it died—therefore nevelah, for there is no shechitah here and it died by itself is considered; but if he returned and slaughtered it properly, behold this is treifah and not nevelah, for shechitah purifies it; in the crop of the esophagus only treifah and not nevelah—and this is correct. And behold if the spinal column was severed in a place not fit for shechitah—the view of Peri Chadash and so too K'li Muor that its law is also like the crop and esophagus—not nevelah; and that which Rambam and Mechaber wrote in seif 1 "behold this is hagramah and invalid" means as they said: it deals with one who did not slaughter again but died from that shechitah; and see Peleti that tzarich iyun, for there is great proof in the spinal column that if majority was severed even not in the place of shechitah it is nevelah—for in any case majority exists and majority is like the whole, as if there is no spinal column at all it is considered; and therefore above where it teaches "leave in the wheat grains"—"invalid" is taught properly and it is nevelah—see there.`,
  '2#ב': `And in a bird etc.—this is the view of the Rambam: all animal and beast share one measure, only in a bird according to value; however the view of Ra'osh is that everything, also in animal, is according to value; and it also depends on this whether we require two fingers or four fingers—and so on at length; only see below siman 41, for there he clarified at length all divided views from Chullin.`,
  '2#ג': `Between a dove—for there are commentators in the Gemara who say yonah, R' Zeira said until Mivlatena, refers to a dove-bird and it has a measure of Mivlatena; and it is not so, for it is a place-name.`,
  '2#ד': `Until the zefek—meaning the roof of the zefek, and not "until" inclusively. And in my view, if he reached the roof of the zefek or slaughtered in the esophagus, he should see whether there are two skins—for in the esophagus there are two skins, unlike the zefek.`,
  '3#א': `And know that he cut—see Peleti, for the view of Shach is a vav of division, and the intent is: or he knew; and in one of them it suffices. And see what I wrote on this: the main thing is the Mechaber switched and holds two are required—return and also must know he cut in the simanim.`,
  '3#ב': `And the same applies to one who slaughters from the nape—meaning also with return and knowing he cut simanim first; but without return, or if it is not clear to him that he slaughtered simanim first, he does not validate—and see Peleti.`,
  '3#ג': `From the nape—and if he did not cut any bone of the spinal column but only slaughtered the simanim, it is obvious it is kosher—for what concern is there? And to say a decree that he not cut the spinal column lest he cut the spinal column—from where do we innovate a decree that we do not find in the Talmud and early authorities? And Rav Berachot Taz was very lenient for us, for we establish one may not slaughter simanim and majority of spinal column—below siman 24; if so, there is no longer concern he will slaughter the nape first and afterward the simanim; he is lenient—and tzarich iyun. But without any cut at all, according to all it is kosher.`,
  '4#_': `It is good to be careful—even when slaughtering opposite the throat and neck—Taz.`,
});

// --- peleti (4) ---
patchFile('siman_020/peleti/part-001.txt', 'peleti', {
  '1#_': `And "that is at the end of the trachea" etc.—and the view of Mahari"l and Mahari"v and the rest of the latter geonim is to be stringent not to slaughter above the large ring; and Beit Chadash wondered: behold the Gemara is arranged until hat slope is kosher; and Shach wrote they relied on what is said in Bava Kamma daf 99 that even though it would be kosher according to Rav, nevertheless they were stringent because of avodah zarah; and this too is so. And it is strained—for there it was within the large ring and discusses the dispute of the rabbis and R. Yehuda ben Beteira; but here above, since the view of R. Yehuda ben Antigonos is that it is kosher—from where do we derive to be stringent? And it appears there is a difficulty if the matters are as they sound simply: did the sages disagree with R. Yehuda ben Antigonos likewise, or that from the ring upward is a place fit for shechitah—and why did they call it hagramah, meaning deror? And Rashi wrote: anything not in the place of shechitah is called hagramah—behold according to R. Yehuda ben Antigonos alone it is a place of shechitah, so why call it hagramah? Therefore he holds that all agree that d'rabbanan it is forbidden to slaughter above the place of the large ring; only if he was derore half outside the ring and half inside—according to R. Yehuda ben Beteira it is treifah because majority in the ring is required; according to R. Yehuda ben Antigonos it is kosher, as with half trachea defective and the place of shechitah is d'oraisa kosher; therefore they called it hagramah for it is outside the place of shechitah—and understand well. It appears at first glance according to what Rashi said in the sugya "one who slaughters within the ring" etc., but in the rest of the rings his slaughter is not kosher in their majority at all except between ring and ring—and Rashi held this on column 2 s.v. lashon shlishi etc., for they wrote; and the first wording appears to me, and there is no obstacle to switch the halakhah, for there is no halakhah in all of them except like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos; and one must understand: if so, why did Rashi trouble to clarify which wording is correct and sharp in the chapter of the Gemara—behold all are mere pilpul; and for practical halakhah it does not stand as Rashi himself said. And especially Tosafot challenged Rashi's explanation that he holds one may not slaughter in the rest of the rings, and explained another explanation; and so in Piskei Tosafot—not like Rashi—that it is permitted to slaughter even within another ring; and all this is unnecessary, for since we establish halakhah like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos, everything falls clearly as Rashi himself said. Therefore it appears: in any case one must understand how R. Yehuda ben Antigonos's testimony that until hat slope is called a place of shechitah—that the rest of the rings that do not surround the entire cavity, as Rashi explained, are fit for shechitah; and if this depends on the large ring being a place of shechitah, nevertheless to slaughter in the ring below it is forbidden because of what Rashi said that they do not surround the entire trachea; if so, one can say that even though R. Yehuda ben Antigonos's testimony is true that the place of shechitah is from hat slope, it is also possible that the rest of the rings are not a place of shechitah. But in truth this is not a difficulty, for from the large ring until hat slope the wheat grains and the like—all do not surround the entire cavity of the trachea, only the majority, and the remainder adhere in the skin and the skin surrounds; and accordingly, since R. Yehuda ben Antigonos said from the large ring until hat slope is fit for shechitah, we learn we do not require that it surround the entire cavity of the spinal column; and how much more so the rest of the rings that are below the large ring, which are called a place of shechitah—and they depend on each other. And accordingly we can understand what we say in the Gemara: R. Zeira ate hagramah of Rav and Shmuel, and the Gemara challenged: he has no stringency of "the place he left" etc.; and the Gemara answered: from Babylonia to Eretz Yisrael it does not apply; or R. Zeira does not intend to return. And one must understand: what is the difficulty—R. Zeira holds as explained there, like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos, that from hat slope is a place of shechitah even though it does not surround its entire cavity; and how much more so hagramah of Rav and Shmuel in the rest of the rings, as said; and one must say: in Babylonia the prohibition of hagramah of Rav and Shmuel in the rest of the rings spread, how much more so above the ring, as said; and therefore it challenged "he has no stringency of the place he left" from there, for even though halakhah is like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos, in Babylonia they practiced prohibition; and it answered he does not intend to return. And accordingly we derive: in Babylonia we do not practice like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos, and even hagramah of Rav and Shmuel in the rest of their rings they practiced to invalidate; and see below chapter Eizehu Mekoman regarding chelev on the maw that they challenged etc., that Rashi wrote: even in a place where the people of Eretz Yisrael and Babylonia disagree, we follow the residents of Babylonia—see there; and accordingly for us there is no R. Yehuda ben Beteira, and even hagramah of Rav and Shmuel is forbidden; and therefore Rashi troubled to clarify what is hagramah of Rav and Shmuel, and clarified the first wording: mainly in the rest of the rings it is forbidden; and he wrote there is no obstacle since halakhah is like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos, only we practice like the people of Babylonia to forbid in any case; from Torah law it is permitted, only by force of custom—and there is no obstacle at all in a mere custom—and understand. Therefore Tosafot challenged Rashi and raised that even for us they forbid like the people of Babylonia—this is the reconciliation; and so Piskei Tosafot—not like Rashi—for us there is a nafka mina that we practice like the people of Babylonia. And with this the words of Rashi in chapter 1 daf 99 in this hagramah that Rav invalidated are explained: Rashi explained he cut within one ring and was derore between ring and ring. And at first glance tzarich iyun: why did Rashi not explain simply as hagramah in Chullin—that he slaughtered, cut the large ring, and was derore above the ring? And also this hagramah that Rabbenu Tam wrote is one explanation that the house of Rashi on the mishnah in the rest of the rings one may not be derore and slaughter between ring and ring; and Rashi rejected that explanation by proof from the tosefta and in Tosafot. And it appears Rashi had a difficulty: we say R. Zeira ate hagramah of Rav and Shmuel and they told him "you are not from the place of Rav and Shmuel," he said "who said it? R. Chiya bar Abba"—and Rashi explained perhaps he heard from another etc.; and this hagramah that came before Rav and he declared treifah is explained that hagramah is forbidden—and it was in a place where R. Chiya bar Abba disagreed, for therefore he exempted the butcher from payment, and Rav and R. Elazar said he did a favor by forbidding it—hence Rav forbids. Therefore Rashi holds this hagramah is not from that, only this other hagramah—meaning all below the large ring: he cut in one ring and was derore between ring and ring. And that he rejected that explanation in the mishnah—certainly Rashi himself held that logically it is called hagramah only he rejected it by proof from the tosefta; and in truth from the tosefta there is no proof, for it is impossible to teach "leave a hair below," for according to the view and explanation of Rashi like Rav and Shmuel the rabbis hold one may not slaughter at all in the rest of the rings, for they are not called trachea at all since they do not surround the entire cavity; if so, how can we say in the tosefta to leave a hair below, for it is not a place of shechitah at all, and it is impossible to slaughter at all within the rest of the rings—and how can hagramah apply since it is impossible to slaughter within it at all? And in any case it is proven that also in the mishnah it is impossible to explain thus; and Rashi who proved from the tosefta and did not prove simply—as said—for it is impossible to slaughter in it and how does majority apply? Because there are three explanations as Rashi said, and for another explanation and wording it is kosher to slaughter within the rest of the rings; therefore Rashi brings proof from the tosefta: whichever explanation it will be, at least from the tosefta it is proven that explanation does not exist. But in truth if we say Rav and Shmuel permit in the rest of the rings like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos, there would be a place of shechitah as R. Zeira holds; if so it is fine to say he slaughtered in other rings and was derore between ring and ring—it is invalid; and this was the incident in the aforementioned hagramah and Rav invalidated it even though one could say it is a place of shechitah—and understand. In any case we see in Babylonia they practiced prohibition not like the testimony of R. Yehuda ben Antigonos, and we follow two customs. And it appears also in Ra'osh one can infer he explains Rav and Shmuel who said even R. Yehuda did not say etc.—meaning majority of the ring does not suffice, rather we require majority of the cavity. And so Tur siman 24 and so in hints; and it is difficult: R. Zeira ate hagramah of Rav and Shmuel—and according to Ra'osh this is majority of the ring in general, and it was unnecessary as I said because he brought stringency from the place he left etc.; we follow the people of Babylonia who are stringent—yet this answer is not sufficient, for if so the norm of the law is that majority of the ring suffices for shechitah, even for treifah if majority of the ring is severed it is treifah; and explicitly we say below in chapter Eizehu Mekoman that Rav Chiya bar Abba checked majority of the cavity of the trachea and it was not so much severed and he validated it, and even Rav ate from it. And see Ramban in Milchamot Hashem who wrote this majority of thickness and majority of cavity applies only in the rest of the rings and not in the large ring—see there. And it is a great difficulty; and one must say that here Ra'osh agrees with R'azah: R. Zeira who ate hagramah—the explanation is not that only majority of the ring was slaughtered—for what relevance is this to the name hagramah; rather it deals with one who slaughtered majority of the ring and afterward was derore outside the large ring—therefore it is called hagramah (and see what Maharsha wrote on this); and R. Zeira who ate did not hold that majority of the ring suffices, only R. Zeira holds like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos that from the large ring upward is also a place of shechitah; if so, what he cut above the ring joins to the majority of the ring and is kosher; if so, on this the Gemara's difficulty applies, and it was unnecessary "stringency of the place he left" etc.; and it answers Abaye and R. Elazar's explanation etc., which teaches explicitly that the people of Babylonia practiced prohibition—and if so, we too follow the people of Babylonia and it is forbidden. And the reason the poskim were not concerned for this—one can say as Rif and Ramban wrote regarding the maw: since the prohibition they practiced in Babylonia is not from the essence of the law but from a corrective enactment, we do not follow it; and even so it appears thus, for there is nothing after the testimony of R. Yehuda ben Antigonos. And it appears more that he holds in what they say "and halakhah is like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos"—Ran agrees with me—see there. One may wonder why Ran mentioned "agrees with me"—behold R. Yehuda, R. Yehuda bar Ilai, and R. Chiya ruled thus; why did Ran mention only Ran? Rather it is simple: practical halakhah is like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos, for there is nothing after his testimony—only this is in Eretz Yisrael; but in Babylonia the prohibition already spread from the instruction of Rav and Shmuel, so that if R. Zeira intended to return he too would have been stringent to forbid; if so, there is no proof from R. Yehuda and R. Yehuda bar Ilai, for all were in Eretz Yisrael; therefore the Gemara brought proof from Ran, for he too was in Babylonia, and nevertheless said halakhah is like R. Yehuda ben Antigonos and did not mention there is prohibition in Babylonia—we learn the prohibition did not spread from Rav and Shmuel—and understand. In any case we derive they ruled well and have a place to rely—Mahariu and Mahari"l—not to slaughter l'chatchila from the large ring; and a slaughterer who slaughters above should be removed, for he is contemptuous and breaches the fence of our rabbinic decisors. And in any case one should be stringent in our times, for in our times we are not expert at all in touching the wheat grains and the like. And last year a student asked the renowned decisor R' Meir Prostitz, who now resides in Schweinfurt, regarding a slaughterer they accused of being derore above the large ring; and because there were many branches I agreed without removing him—and were it not for this, my view was to remove him; and do not forsake your mother's teaching.`,
  '2#_': `Without forcing itself—in the Gemara daf 45 we say: anything that stretches its neck and grazes only if it does not force itself—Rashi explained: by a person; and the Gemara asks: if it forced itself—what? Rashi explained: that it stretched its neck excessively at the time of shechitah; teiku; and R. Yehuda and R. Yehuda bar Ilai derived the matter between them: forced in the simanim and slaughtered—invalid—end; and Peri Chadash wrote from this it implies every doubt is in forcing itself; but if a person forced it—certainly invalid; and the Rambam in chapter 1 of laws of shechitah included in one: whether it forced itself or a person forced the simanim and he slaughtered—behold it is doubtful nevelah—end; and Peri Chadash challenged that forcing by a person is not in the category of doubt. And it appears the Rambam holds: certainly if he forced so much in the simanim to pull them out as it would extend if the animal extended—if it forced itself, certainly kosher; for what he wrote "and what he wrote animal" is only if it extended excessively in this—certainly invalid; and this is our doubt, for it is unknown whether the animal was extending by its forcing or not; therefore the Rambam included them in one.`,
  '3#_': `His slaughter is invalid—and so wherever it says invalid, it means nevelah; and Shach and Peri Chadash raised that certainly in the crop of the esophagus there is no nevelah, only it deals with one who did not yet slaughter it; and since this shechitah is not called shechitah, behold it is as if it died by itself—and obviously it is nevelah; therefore Peri Chadash raised: if he returned and slaughtered it afterward properly, this shechitah purifies immediately from nevelah and behold it is only treifah and not nevelah, for the crop is only treifah and shechitah purifies from nevelah—and this is correct and one should not doubt; see what I wrote above siman 33 at length on this—only what Peri To'ar wrote in s.k. 5, and so K'li Muor's view: if majority of the trachea was severed above in a place not fit for shechitah—it is only treifah; this matter tzarich iyun in my humble opinion, for besides what Peri Chadash already stood on—why did the Rambam not count among the 70 treifot a severed spinal column above the place of shechitah, as he counted perforation of the crop and esophagus, that it is only treifah and not nevelah, and he distinguished this from that? Also we say Chullin daf 19: he was derore a third and slaughtered a third—Rabbi Yehuda said kosher, for life departs with shechitah; and so this reasoning is said in several places; and it is difficult: if you say in severing the trachea not in the place of shechitah it is not nevelah but treifah—we learn life does not depart in that severing, for it is only treifah; if so, how does life depart in deroring a third and slaughtering a third, and how can shechitah be validated—for the third he slaughtered in the place of shechitah there is no majority, and it is kosher; and the third he slaughtered above the place of shechitah—there life does not depart, for above the place of shechitah it is only treifah; if so, from every side life does not depart at all, and in the Gemara they said life departs—how can shechitah be validated if he did not make an act of nevelah? Therefore it is clear: since majority of the spinal column was severed and majority is like the whole in all the Torah, it is as if removed and the spinal column was severed—and nevelah—and understand. And what the Rambam wrote in chapter 3 of laws of shechitah halakhah 22: spinal column severed, majority of its cavity in a place fit for shechitah—it is nevelah—K'li Muor wanted to infer from this that not in the place of shechitah is treifah; this inference is not a complete inference, for we establish that even in a place fit for shechitah we require majority of its cavity and majority of thickness does not suffice; and see Ramban in his Milchamot chapter 1 of Chullin in the sugya "one who slaughters within the ring"—see there; and this inference is not worth rejecting the aforementioned matters, whose foundation is in clear proof—and this is clear.`,
  '4#_': `And know that he cut them etc.—the words of Shulchan Aruch are difficult to reconcile; and Shach already stood on this in s.k. 8 at length and confused the reader until it is hard to stand on it. And I will bring briefly: let us set aside the words of Tosafot and grasp only Ra'osh's words, who wrote on the mishnah "one who slaughters from the sides—it is kosher," that it deals with one who knows he cut the simanim before he cut the spinal column. And Rav Beit Yosef explained his words that nevertheless we require return of simanim too—and this is not so, for then there is still difficulty: what difference is there between from the sides and slaughtering opposite the nape—for in both, if he returned it is kosher? Accordingly, with knowing, even if he did not return it is kosher—for it could be the simanim were there and he slaughtered in them first, unlike opposite the nape: even if he knew, since he did not return it is invalid—for it says explicitly he knows, which is impossible to know at all—and understand. And if he returned, one can say Ra'osh holds in the default case and does not distinguish at all with return that knowledge too is required; and one should not challenge: if so, why did the Gemara infer in melikah that return is not needed, for otherwise melikah of the nape would be kosher—accordingly with return of simanim, if so slaughter too would be kosher? What difficulty—for perhaps it deals with one who did not know, and return does not help to validate the slaughter—this is not difficult, for it is similar to the beginning "one who slaughters from the sides"; accordingly it deals with knowing, as Ra'osh said; if so, at the end too it deals in this manner, and nevertheless invalid—and this is clear. If so, Shulchan Aruch who wrote "and know that he cut them" etc. did not need to write that he returned—for according to Ra'osh's words return is not needed. And if we explain as Shach wrote: "and know" is a vav of division, as if he said: or he knows etc.—this too is not settled, for Shulchan Aruch concluded "and the same applies to one who slaughters from the nape," and for the nape knowing without return does not help, as written; and behold Tosafot wrote: from the sides—we find to say specifically he did; but l'chatchila, even if he returned simanim, decree lest he not return; and with this Maharsha explained he holds the mishnah deals in all cases of return and no return, and in all of them specifically he did; and this is not decree upon decree, for one can say they are one decree. And in the default case they decreed not to slaughter from the sides l'chatchila; and it is possible even with knowing that he slaughters simanim too is included in the decree; and Rav Beit Yosef answered Tosafot who invalidate b'dieved if he did not return simanim; and Shach explained this means he did not know he cut in the simanim first—for if he knew, return is not needed, unlike the nape where return is needed in every case; and this is the essence of Shach's words. If so, one must still say: or he knew, as the Mechaber—and still difficult why with knowing alone on the nape it is kosher, for we require return too—and tzarich iyun. Therefore one must say the Mechaber certainly holds like Tosafot that we require return—for without return it is invalid; only with return Tosafot do not require, in their view, that he know he slaughtered simanim first, for from the default case he slaughtered them first, as Hagahot Ra'osh wrote; but nevertheless on the nape return does not help unless he knows clearly that he slaughtered simanim first; and if challenged: if so, why does the Gemara challenge according to the one who holds even in melikah he requires etc.—what difficulty, for perhaps it deals with one who did not know? One must say: just as in melikah certainly he must know clearly that he did not perform melikah on the simanim first but on the spinal column—for that is the essence of the mitzvah of melikah—if so, learn from it in shechitah: since the mitzvah of shechitah is in the simanim, he must know he slaughtered simanim first; so too one must say for this view. And the Mechaber holds Ra'osh and Tosafot disagree: Tosafot hold knowledge is not needed, only return suffices; and Ra'osh holds there is no nafka mina in return, only knowledge suffices; and the Mechaber is stringent like both—that return and knowledge are required—to satisfy both views; and this is also for the nape kosher, since there is return and knowledge—and understand.`,
});

// --- kaf-hachayim (4) ---
patchFile('siman_020/kaf-hachayim/part-001.txt', 'kaf-hachayim', {
  '1#_': `<b>(a) [Seif 1] The place of shechitah in the neck in the trachea toward the head from hat slope downward, etc.; and that is, leave in the wheat grains, etc.</b> Our master the Ba'al HaTurim did not mention here nor in siman 24 "leave in the wheat grains"; and so Rashba in teshuvah ha'arokhah and Semak; and he wrote that from what is said daf 45a—it is possible because they were in doubt about the explanation of wheat grains, what it is and how far it extends and what is called "leave in the wheat grains." And in Sefer Yair Netiv it is written: the matter of these wheat grains—we are not expert; and I too searched and investigated and asked the mouths of the great inspectors expert in this craft properly, and they answered me they did not reach a conclusion, etc.; therefore one who fears the word of Hashem will not validate any hagramah that is above the large ring, since we are not expert and do not know what these wheat grains are or where they are; and so R' Yechiel: a slaughterer should distance himself from the large ring and slaughter below, even though it is simple to us that the entire neck is fit for shechitah, in order to remove doubt—end. And so commanded Mahari Segal not to validate at all from the large ring; and so Agur in Ashkenazi shechitot writes they practice to declare treifah for hagramah if he did not leave the full thread in the large ring toward the head across its entire face, as the rabbis of R. Yehuda bar Yehuda; and so I saw practiced Mahari Landau; and so the custom spread in all the provinces—end. Kenesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Tur part 2; and so Peri To'ar part 1: for practical halakhah, since nowadays we are not expert in wheat grains and it is also impossible to narrow the beginning of hat slope, it appears one must slaughter within the large ring and leave at least a hair's breadth from the large ring toward the head across the majority of the ring—and aside from the law that it is not called shechitah; and so Rambam, Ra'osh, Rashba, and so is the halakhah—end. And Rav Zivchei Tzedek wrote part 1: one should rule as Agur's words; and so they practice in the city of Baghdad—see there: if nothing remained of the large ring toward the head across its entire face they declare the animal treifah, only in a place of great loss; and how much more so for a great need, if the majority of the ring remained they validate it—see there.`,
  '2#_': `<b>(b) A slaughterer</b> who slaughters above the large ring should be removed, for he is contemptuous and breaches the fence of our rabbinic decisors—Peleti part 1; and so Chayei Adam general rules 6 part 1: some say he should be removed and fined—Zivchei Tzedek part 2.`,
  '3#_': `<b>(c) There. And its measure below until the head of the wing of the lung, etc.</b> That it is on the left side; and when the animal grazes it protrudes more from the body than the right side, which is thicker—Beit Yosef in the name of Hagahot Maimoniyot; Shach s.k. 2; Peri Chadash part 3; Zivchei Tzedek part 2; and this measure is for stringency—Sha'arei Dura part 2; Zivchei Tzedek part 3; but Kometz part 2 wrote: if the cut in the trachea is found above that point—even so it is kosher; and if equal—this is hagramah; and he wrote that this is the implication of all the commentators—see there.`,
  '4#_': `<b>(d) And it appears</b> that one estimates in this place without pulling the trachea forcibly upward, for on the side where the trachea by nature wrinkles somewhat—if one pulls the trachea forcibly, the great trachea that reaches there to the head of the wing of the lung will be pulled upward and come to leniency—Peri To'ar part 2; Zivchei Tzedek part 3; Minchat Yitzchak part 3; Zivchei Tzedek part 4.`,
});

// --- rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd (1) ---
patchFile('siman_020/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt', 'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd', {
  '1#_': `(Siman 20 seif 3) <b>And the same applies to one who slaughters from the nape.</b> And without holding and he slaughtered the spinal column and simanim without flesh—there is to examine for practical halakhah whether it is treifah or nevelah from the reason of chaladah (slaughter by hiding), for in the sugya we say: Ze'ira said: we too taught etc.; Rabbi Yehuda said: because he inserts (machlid); Rava said: because he presses (dores). The one who says because he presses—what is the reason? The other says because he inserts. He tells you: chaladah—what is it? Like a weasel that returns to the foundations of houses. If so, for us in such a case without majority flesh, it depends on the dispute of Rabbi Yehuda and R. Chiya bar Abba: for Rabbi Yehuda it is chaladah and invalid in shechitah; for R. Chiya bar Abba with melikah and bringing it is only treifah from breaking the spinal column before the simanim—and see Semag; and it is below (siman 24 seif 9) one must be careful, etc.; and its foundation is from that which Rashi explained in Zevachim in "because he inserts"—it is explained he holds halakhah is like Rabbi Yehuda, or he is in doubt and it is nevelah. And nevertheless it appears in my humble opinion we establish like Rabbi Yehuda, for we say: the one who says because he presses holds melikah and bringing is invalid; and we establish melikah and bringing is kosher, as Rambam wrote (chapter 6 of laws of korbanot)—it is proven the reason is because he inserts; and even though we establish like Ze'ira, nevertheless we conclude that the mitzvah of melikah is without majority flesh, and therefore we need the reason of inserting. And see in Tosafot in the sugya who wrote two explanations in "because he inserts": for their first answer—this is with a knife that is not wide specifically; and for their second answer—it is chaladah because he slaughters the simanim from below upward. For both answers I wonder: if so, how do we challenge the one who says because he presses—why does the other not say because he inserts? For their first answer it is difficult: since he is in doubt about melikah and bringing invalid, he should have mentioned more the truth "because he presses," for invalid even with a wide knife. And for their second answer it is difficult: perhaps this one who says holds even with return, as the sons of R. Chiya hold above; if so, the reason of pressing is a decided matter, since he holds melikah and bringing invalid; but the reason of chaladah is not a decided matter, for with return of simanim that he slaughters the simanim properly there is no chaladah. And similarly I wonder on what Rashba wrote in chapter 2 (daf 30): they say in the house of Rav "under the skin" etc.; and they say in the house of Rav Rabbi Yehuda said because he inserts—it is resolved that under the skin is chaladah [and this is not like Tosafot's second answer above]; and he answered: Rabbi Yehuda is sharp, for there is no support for Ze'ira, for perhaps "because he inserts" means perhaps in truth under the skin is chaladah. But according to the truth that exists for Ze'ira there is no proof it is chaladah—see there [and at first glance it is wondrous: for even according to the conclusion that exists for Ze'ira, we conclude the mitzvah of melikah cuts the spinal column and spinal process without majority flesh; if so, the difficulty of "let a knife purify it from nevelah" still applies for me, and we need the reason because he inserts. One must say Rabbi Yehuda did not know this baraita of melikah without majority flesh, as Rashba wrote in the sugya that R. Yehuda and R. Elazar did not know this baraita. However I wonder: if so, for practical halakhah it should be ruled that under the skin is certainly chaladah, for according to the truth that the mitzvah of melikah is without majority flesh, if so one who performs melikah with a knife becomes impure in the tent, and a knife does not help to purify from nevelah—for it is certainly impossible to say that with a knife usually majority flesh is also cut; if so, how do we challenge in the sugya (daf 28a) "and if you say there is no shechitah for a bird from the Torah, let a knife help him" etc.—for according to what we establish like Ze'ira it is nevelah before reaching the simanim; therefore the challenge is from the fact that the mitzvah of melikah is without majority flesh and he performs melikah with a knife also without majority flesh. If so, we need according to the truth the reason of Rabbi Yehuda and Rava. And since we establish melikah and bringing is kosher, it is proven the reason is because he inserts, as said; if so, it is proven for practical halakhah that under the skin is chaladah—and tzarich iyun]—if so, what do they challenge: the one who says because he presses—why does the other not say because he inserts? For since according to him in truth it is invalid because he presses, he did not want to say because he inserts, lest it be inferred from him that under the skin is chaladah—and tzarich iyun.`,
});

